. rv_out Tab6_replications, landscape ///
>     title("Table 6. Institutions and government size: replications with contemporaneous and predetermined measures") ///
>     notes("Dependent variable: growth of real GDP per capita (%) in panels A–C; growth of real GDP (%) in panel D. Panels A and B follow the panel specification of Oto-Peralías and Romero-Ávila (201
> 3, working-paper version INFER 2012/3): five-year averages, growth on government final consumption (% of GDP, WDI), institutional quality and their interaction, with initial log GDP per capita, seco
> ndary school enrolment and investment, and period effects. Their ICRG bureaucracy-quality index is proprietary; panel A uses the public ICRG quality-of-government index distributed by QoG (0–1, from
>  1984) and panel B the WGI government effectiveness estimate (from 1996), as specified in the analysis plan; legal origin, one of their excluded instruments, is not available and is dropped. System 
> GMM: two-step, collapsed instruments, consumption, interaction and investment endogenous (lags 2 and deeper), income, enrolment and institutions predetermined (lags 1 and deeper), period dummies and
>  the population dependency ratio as standard instruments; Windmeijer-corrected standard errors. Fixed effects: country and period fixed effects, standard errors clustered by country. Only the timing
>  of government consumption changes across column pairs (period mean, first year of the period, last year of the previous period); each panel uses the same sample for all measures. A positive interac
> tion means that better institutions mitigate the growth cost of government size. Panel C: annual panel 1996–2019 (the window of Mollick and Vianna, 2024), government consumption interacted with the
>  mean of the six WGI indicators (centred), country and year fixed effects, lagged growth and the controls of Table 1, Driscoll-Kraay standard errors (maximum lag 3); this is the specification regist
> ered in the analysis plan, written when the published text was not available, and it is an approximate replication only. Panel D (exploratory, added after the plan when the text was obtained): the p
> ublished specification of Mollick and Vianna (2024, Table 2), with growth of real GDP (%) on its lag, log government consumption, WGI government effectiveness, their interaction, log investment and 
> population growth, country and year fixed effects, heteroskedasticity-robust (White) standard errors, their 138 countries; only the measure of government consumption changes across columns, on the s
> ame sample. Full results in Table S17. * p<0.10, ** p<0.05, *** p<0.01.")
